---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CustomisedBreadCrumbs from "../components/CustomisedBreadCrumbs.astro";

const thread = [
  {
    href: "/projects/asd-myelin-timing",
    kicker: "Myelin",
    line: "A lipid-supply idea, tested, and narrowed to timing.",
  },
  {
    href: "/projects/anesthesia-pipeline",
    kicker: "Anesthesia and sleep",
    line: "What a reduced-activity state shares with sleep, and what lets it reverse.",
  },
  {
    href: "/projects/libribrain-2026",
    kicker: "LibriBrain",
    line: "Which of fifty words was heard, and how much of the score is only the clock.",
  },
  {
    href: "/projects/handwriting-emg",
    kicker: "Handwriting",
    line: "Wrist EMG, 30.07% character error reproduced, 15.02% after the controls.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Rohit Ravi"
  description="Rohit Ravi is an undergraduate researcher at NYU's Center for Data Science. An unexplained illness, and a young relative who cannot speak, are why the neural-data work exists."
>
  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-10">
    <CustomisedBreadCrumbs />

    <header class="mt-8 grid items-end gap-8 md:grid-cols-[17rem_minmax(0,1fr)]">
      <figure class="mx-auto w-full max-w-xs md:mx-0 md:max-w-none">
        <img
          src="/images/about/rohit-ravi.jpg"
          alt="Rohit Ravi, in a navy suit, standing between stone columns."
          width="1024"
          height="1536"
          class="aspect-[3/4] w-full rounded-lg object-cover object-[center_18%] shadow-md"
        />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-xs text-muted-foreground md:text-left">
          Rohit Ravi
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-muted-foreground">About</p>
        <h1 class="mt-2 scroll-m-20 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Rohit Ravi</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed">
          Undergraduate researcher at the Center for Data Science, Courant Institute, New York
          University. Data science, with a minor in philosophy.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The work on this site is machine learning on neural recordings, and on nervous systems
          that look intact and still fail. It is one trail. It started because that was my
          situation, and then because it was a child's.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <nav class="mt-10 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2" aria-label="The work this page leads to">
      {
        thread.map((item) => (
          <a
            href={item.href}
            class="rounded-lg border bg-card px-4 py-3 transition hover:border-primary"
          >
            <span class="block text-sm font-semibold text-primary">{item.kicker}</span>
            <span class="mt-1 block text-sm leading-snug text-muted-foreground">{item.line}</span>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </nav>

    <div class="mt-12 flex flex-col gap-12 text-base leading-relaxed">
      <section>
        <h2 class="border-t pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Sophomore year</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
          <p>
            The illness arrived in the first month of sophomore year. For a stretch there was no
            interval between attacks. Pain and vomiting ran together, and the ordinary machinery
            of a semester, class, food, sleep, stopped being available. I saw specialists across
            the relevant fields. Several of them added something to the regimen that helped. The
            episodes became less frequent. Between them I could work again.
          </p>
          <p>
            What none of them could give me was a cause. Years later that is still the open fact.
            The condition is manageable. It is not explained. Scans and endoscopies had found
            nothing structurally wrong, which is a strange kind of news. It rules out a lesion
            you can point at, and it leaves the question of where a nervous system is failing
            when the anatomy looks ordinary.
          </p>
          <p>
            I did not have a lab. I had coursework in machine learning, and I had
            <em>C. elegans</em>, a nervous system small enough that every cell and every wire is
            named. The coursework was a way of keeping the question in a form that could be
            wrong. If nothing is broken in the picture, the failure might still be genetic. It
            might be mitochondrial, a cell that cannot pay for its own maintenance. Or the wires
            might be fine and the failure might sit next to them, in the insulation. Myelin is
            mostly lipid. A circuit can look intact on a scan and still conduct late, thin, or
            out of time.
          </p>
          <p>
            Reading in that direction, I tried two things on myself that were not part of any
            protocol I had been given. DHA helped. Palmitoylethanolamide with luteolin helped. I
            am reporting that as my own experience. I am not recommending either compound, and I
            never ran the comparison that would say how much of the change was them and how much
            was the rest of what I was already taking. DHA has also been reported to help some
            autistic children. That report, set next to what I had felt, is what turned a private
            note about lipids into a project.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="border-t pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">A relative, six years old</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
          <p>
            I have a relative who is six. He cannot speak, and he is in pain much of the time.
            I knew that kind of pain from the inside, and seeing it on him is what made the
            question urgent. I wanted to understand the machinery. I did not set out to propose
            a treatment, and I am not second-guessing the clinicians who take care of him.
          </p>
          <p>
            The idea I wrote down before running anything was specific enough to fail. If the
            supply of lipid is constitutionally weak, the circuits that myelin latest should be
            the ones that fail first, and in people those are frontal. Autism liability and
            white-matter microstructure can be compared in public biobank data, so the shape of
            that prediction could be checked without waiting on a new experiment. The
            <a href="/projects/asd-myelin-timing" class="underline hover:font-bold">myelin page</a>
            is the log. The original form is not supported. The sharing with autism liability is
            real as a weak global trend and absent as a frontal gradient. What survived is
            narrower: a question about the timing of conduction, not a shortage of insulation.
            None of that helps a six-year-old who still cannot speak. It was never expected to.
            It is a sharper question than the one I started with, and it is the one I can
            actually work.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="border-t pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Quiet, and the return from it</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
          <p>
            In the worst episodes the pain has stopped under sedation deep enough to count as
            anesthesia. I am leaving the names of those drugs off this site. The fact is enough
            to force a question that is no longer about my chart. A nervous system can be driven
            down into a quiet state and brought back. Sleep is another quiet state. So are the
            reduced-activity states a small animal enters on its own. It is not obvious which
            parts of the quiet are shared, or what has to remain true for the return to be
            possible.
          </p>
          <p>
            The
            <a href="/projects/anesthesia-pipeline" class="underline hover:font-bold">anesthesia page</a>
            asks that on a connectome. The model has to say, before the result is looked at, what
            a real anesthetic does to the network and what a lookalike compound that does not
            anesthetize fails to do. A number of the early claims on that page have been
            withdrawn in public, on the page itself. The claims that remain are the ones that
            were still standing after the withdrawal. That is the standard I want the rest of
            the work held to. An explanation that cannot survive contact with its own measurement
            is the kind of explanation I had already been given.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="border-t pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What someone else can score</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
          <p>
            The recent decoding work is the same habit carried into data another person can
            rerun. It is also the part a lab or a company can grade without taking my word for
            the rest of this page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/projects/libribrain-2026" class="underline hover:font-bold">LibriBrain</a>
            asks which of fifty words a listener heard, read from the magnetic field over the
            scalp. The entry finished eighth on the broad public track. A baseline that uses only
            the timing of the recording, and none of the field, scores 0.632. The full decoder
            is higher. A large piece of what presents as neural decoding is the clock. I would
            rather be known for that finding than for the rank.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/projects/handwriting-emg" class="underline hover:font-bold">Handwriting from the wrist</a>
            is the other current piece. Meta released a model that reads letters from surface EMG.
            On their ten held-out users I reproduced their character error, 30.07%. A fine-tune on
            the writer's own data, together with a language model that had been stripped of any
            prompt it might meet at test time, reaches 15.02%. The two gains add. They are not
            one gain counted twice. Fine-tuning on other writers makes the model worse, which is
            how you know the improvement belongs to the person and not to a leak in the split.
          </p>
          <p>
            Neither project treats the illness, and neither is aimed at the boy. They are the
            public end of a private question. When an explanation is missing, which measurements
            are still worth trusting? I want to be hired to make those measurements. Neural data,
            machine learning, and the willingness to publish the number that failed. The reason
            the work is aimed at nervous systems, and at people who are still waiting for an
            account of what is happening to them, is the illness, and him.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
